Yes. Indigo Hammer vacuum is the right move here: evacuate the interpretation and see what remains physically true. Then reintroduce meaning only after we know which structures survive.

Strip away raven, oracle, Pantheon, message, intention, TikTok. What remains is:

\boxed{\text{history} \rightarrow \text{structure} \rightarrow \text{interaction} \rightarrow \text{observable signal}}

That invariant is real across all three examples.

A feather is not merely a decorative surface. It is a hierarchical material whose keratin, air spaces, melanosomes, geometry, orientation, and pigmentation determine how incoming light is absorbed, scattered, and reflected. In structurally colored feathers, nanometer-scale organization can behave like an optical structure—constructive interference and related effects make particular wavelengths conspicuous. Researchers explicitly study these biological architectures as inspiration for engineered photonic materials and structural-color patterning. 

So your “lithography” analogy is close, with a qualification: the raven isn’t known to be consciously lithographically writing messages into its feathers. But development is manufacturing a patterned optical medium. Change microscopic organization and you change what an observer gets back from the same incident light.

And feathers contain another channel entirely.

While a feather grows, environmental and biological information gets incorporated into it. Stable-isotope ratios and some contaminants can become effectively fixed once deposited, while growth bars preserve aspects of feather-growth timing. Researchers can therefore use feathers as historical records rather than treating them merely as surfaces. 

Hair has the same remarkable property. Keratinized hair grows sequentially and does not remodel afterward in the way living tissue does, so sequential segments can preserve temporally ordered chemical/isotopic information. Elemental and isotope analysis can therefore reconstruct aspects of exposure or diet across time, although contamination and interpretation are important limitations. 

That’s why your chemical khipu metaphor works surprisingly well:

